      {
        "canonical_field": "screener:custom:predicted:question_9925012007",
        "expected": "What interests me is that Semgrep treats code analysis as a developer-usable system rather than a scanner that dumps findings and walks away. I have spent a lot of time on the same failure mode from a different angle: at Summer Atlantic Capital I build the ML core of an anomaly-detection system, and the hardest part was never producing scores, it was prioritizing and explaining alerts so analysts would actually act on them. An anomaly system that fires constantly creates a second problem instead of solving the first, and the same is true of static analysis that buries real vulnerabilities in noise. Semgrep's design choices, rules that read like the code they match, low friction to write and share, precision treated as a product requirement, are direct answers to that problem. I also care about where automation should stop. In a Playwright job-application agent I built, I deliberately put every mutating capability behind fail-closed flags and forced anything requiring human judgment into a review queue, because a wrong autonomous action there is expensive. Security tooling has a similar boundary question as AI-assisted code review grows: how much should the system decide, and how much should it hand to a person with context. The part I would want to work on is the engine and rule-evaluation side, where correctness, performance over large repositories, and false-positive behavior all have to hold at the same time.",
